WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.320
YUFENG GUO: NumPy is fast, but
how can we make it even faster?

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.371
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:06.371 --> 00:00:09.317
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.340
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.190
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.710
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.620
we're going to look
at a new library

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:30.330
from Google Research called
JAX and see how it can speed up

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.830
machine learning.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:36.240
JAX can automatically
differentiate native Python

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.520
and NumPy functions.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.700
It can differentiate through
loops, branches, recursion,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.020
and closures.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.430
And it can take derivatives
of derivatives of derivatives.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.580
It supports reverse mode
differentiation, also known

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:55.440
as back-propagation, using
the grad function, as well as

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.330
forward mode differentiation.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.360
And the two can be
composed arbitrarily

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.890
in any order you want.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.250
It can seem like every other
library these days supports

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.370
auto-differentiation.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.280
So what else can JAX do?

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.140
Well, it's also able to speed
up your code, sometimes really

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.470
significantly, by using
a special compiler

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.400
under the hood.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.030
Accelerated Linear
Algebra, or XLA,

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:26.050
is a domain-specific
compiler for linear algebra.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.810
It can perform optimizations,
like fusing operations together

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.780
so intermediate
results don't need

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.730
to be written out to memory.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.160
Instead of this
time-consuming process,

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.550
this data gets streamed right
into the next operation.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:43.560
And this enables faster and
more efficient processing.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.730
JAX uses XLA to compile
and run your NumPy programs

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.280
on GPUs and TPUs.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.620
Compilation happens
transparently,

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.320
with NumPy library
calls getting sped up.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.430
But JAX takes it a step
further than XLA alone,

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:02.340
as it lets you just-in-time
compile your very own Python

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.070
functions into
XLA-optimized kernels,

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.820
using a single function
API called jit.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.430
Compilation and
automatic differentiation

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.530
can be composed
arbitrarily, so you

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.810
can express
sophisticated algorithms

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:20.250
and get maximum performance,
all without leaving Python.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.230
What else is there
other than jit?

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.030
There's also pmap.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.940
Applying pmap means that
the function you write

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.490
gets compiled by
XLA, just like jit,

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:33.730
and replicated and then executed
in parallel across devices.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.090
That's what the p
in pmap stands for.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.600
This means you can do
compilations on multiple GPUs

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.810
or TPU cores all
at once using pmap,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.400
and then differentiate
through them all.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.530
JAX boils down to an extensible
system for composable function

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:50.880
transformations.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.460
The main ones today are grad,
jit, pmap, and also vmap.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.820
vmap is used for
automatic vectorization,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.580
allowing you to turn a function
that can handle only one data

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.520
point into a function that can
handle a batch of these data

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.905
points of any size with just
a single wrapper function.

00:03:09.905 --> 00:03:11.280
Let's take a look
at how this all

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.740
comes together using
a familiar example,

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:17.820
training a deep neural network
on the [? MNIST ?] data set.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.880
This notebook starts out by
creating two utility functions

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.190
to make a neural network
with just randomly

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:24.630
initialized parameters.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.520
I printed out the dimensions
of each layer for convenience.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:31.170
We can see here that it
takes a 784-unit-wide input

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:35.490
and passes through two hidden
layers whose size is 512 each.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.890
And the outputs are
the usual 10 classes,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.990
since we're predicting
what digit is

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.602
supposed to be in that image.

00:03:43.602 --> 00:03:45.060
Next, we have a
function that takes

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.270
care of running an image
through our predict function.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.240
Our predict function only
handles one image at a time.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.920
And we can confirm
this by passing

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.550
in a single random image of
the correct dimensions, which

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.340
gives us a vector of
size 10, representing

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.190
the 10 logit values coming
out of the final layer

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:03.630
of the network.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.200
But when we try a whole batch
of images-- say of length 10--

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.130
also, it fails, since the array
dimensions no longer line up.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:14.790
But we're in luck, because
wrapping our predict function

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:18.480
in a vmap will allow us to
take advantage of matrix

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.790
multiplication and
run all 10 images

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:24.120
through the model in a single
pass, rather than doing them

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.630
one by one.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.240
The resultant
function can handle

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.880
a batch of arbitrary
size, and we don't have

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.770
to modify our function one bit.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:36.840
Notice that the output is now
10 by 10, representing the 10

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.810
logit values coming out of the
final layer for each of the 10

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.350
examples in that batch.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.470
Now let's see how we can use
the grad and jit functions

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.390
to build out the
remainder of our model,

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:48.943
as well as our training code.

00:04:48.943 --> 00:04:50.360
We'll add in a
function to one_hot

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.520
encode our data, and a
couple more functions

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.920
to calculate accuracy and loss.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.840
Finally, we'll put together
our update function,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.870
which will take the result
of the loss function

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:02.060
and run grad on it,
which will take care

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:04.700
of the back-propagation for
us and return the updated

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.110
parameters of the model.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.500
Now we're almost ready
to run our model.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.150
We just need to add
in some code using

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:14.210
TensorFlow data sets to bring
in our [? MNIST ?] data set.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.930
JAX purposely does not include
data set loading functionality,

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.030
as it's focused on
program transformations

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.000
and accelerator-backed NumPy.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.120
So now we're ready
to train our model.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.430
Our training loop is
set for 10 epochs.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.690
And we have a timer
added in as well,

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.760
because we want to
see how it performs.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:34.840
So let's run this.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.630
And we can see that
across 10 epochs,

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:40.410
we ended up spending about
22 seconds per epoch.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.365
Now, you might be
thinking, wait, wait,

00:05:42.365 --> 00:05:43.740
didn't Yufeng
mentioned something

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.330
about using the jit function?

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.880
Did we ever add that?

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:48.030
Good catch.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:51.510
Let's add in the @jit decorator
at the top of our update

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.930
function, and we'll
rename it jit_update.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.110
Now we'll have a before
and after comparison.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:59.610
Let's re-initialize
our parameters

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.070
using the same
init_network_params function

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:05.260
we used earlier, and then
run our new training loop.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:08.010
And we'll see how
the timing works out.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:11.100
So this is looking like
it's taking way less time--

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.770
only 8 seconds per epoch.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.620
And all we had to do
was add four characters

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.120
to the top of the update loop.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.150
Now that's "le-jit."

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.340
Before I close this out,
I want to remind viewers

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.710
that as of this
recording, JAX is still

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:29.130
just a research project and
not an official Google product.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:32.500
So it's likely you may
encounter bugs and sharp edges.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.940
The team has even made a list of
gotchas and a gotchas notebook

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.020
to help you out.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.175
Since this list will
be constantly evolving,

00:06:39.175 --> 00:06:40.800
be sure to see what
the state of things

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.453
are if you plan on using
jit for your project.

